  "1491": "A groundbreaking study that radically alters our understanding of the Americas before the arrival of the Europeans in 1492.",
  "1493": "From the author of 1491 -- the best-selling study of the pre-Columbian Americas -- a deeply engaging new history of the most momentous biological event since the death of the dinosaurs.",
  "1984": "Winston Smith lives in a society where the government controls people every second of the day. He fights this world with love. But it's dangerous: love for another person can be punished by death - and Big Brother is always watching.",
  "An Incomplete Education": "A witty single-volume crash course through the major fields every well-rounded person is supposed to have learned — and probably forgot.",
  "Engineering in Plain Sight": "Grady Hillhouse’s illustrated field guide to the hidden infrastructure all around us: power grids, pipelines, cell towers and more.",
  "Seven and a Half Lessons About the Brain": "Have you ever wondered why you have a brain? Let renowned neuroscientist Lisa Feldman Barrett demystify that big gray blob between your ears.",
  "12 Rules for Life": "Jordan Peterson draws on psychology, mythology and clinical practice to offer twelve principles for living with responsibility and meaning amid chaos.",
  "21 Lessons for the 21st Century": "In a world deluged by irrelevant information, clarity is power. Censorship works not by blocking the flow of information, but rather by flooding people with disinformation and distractions.",
  "The 48 Laws of Power": "Amoral, cunning, ruthless, and instructive, this piercing work distills three thousand years of the history of power in to forty-eight well explicated laws.",
  "A Conflict of Visions": "Controversies in politics arise from many sources, but the conflicts which endure for generations or for centuries show a remarkably consistent pattern.",
  "Against Empathy": "\"We often think of our capacity to experience the suffering of others as the ultimate source of goodness. Many of our wisest policy-makers, activists, scientists, and philosophers agree that the only problem with empathy is that we don't have enough of it.",
